\f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 HeartTheft
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 Chapter 5 \endash  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 Intoxicated Dreams}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
\par 
\par Dear Father, 
\par I know I am sending letters ad nauseum now, but you must forgive me for taking up so much of your tim
e. I need to make Confession and I cannot do so at any Church near to my current location that is approved by the Ministry of Inquisition for disclosing sensitive information. I am thereby relegated, once again, to doing so via post.
\par My last letter was hastily scrawled}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  the morning I lost her}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
, and I was not able to afford a swift Registered Courier, so I hope it made it to you safely. Despite my humiliating failure to capture her at the carriage house, I had assumed that in a few days\rquote  time, I\rquote d be well on her trail again
, and not delivering you further bad news. But regrettably, that is not the case. 
\par As I write this, I find myself lost, sheltering from the elements with little to no coin to my name in the loft of a dilapidated barn. Additionally and most shamefully, I have allowed myself to fall intoxicated on country wine
, while reading worldly literature I am certain you would not approve of.
\par God finds ways to humble us, when we grow arrogant. You always said that.
\par Pray, let me explain.
\par  From the moment she left me there locked in her room, I set about making as much noise banging on the door and the floors to rouse the servant staff as was possible without actually risking injury}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , or destroying my accommodations}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 . As you are well aware, I can be a loud man when I want to}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  be}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 .
\par Despite that, perhaps because I was in the farthest room from the servants quarters below on the lower floor, and additionally the carriage house was apparently not fully occupied, it took far too long for anyone to show up with a set of keys. 
My rescuers were one of the sleepy-eyed maids and an aged, large boar who I think I later found out was the cook, playing bodyguard for the lady with the house keys likely because they were concerned I was galloping drunk, or otherwise not of sound mind. 

\par Once released and after hurrying to my room so that I could show my Ordination Patents, the nightmare continued. Apparently, in my dazed rush to pursue the Heart Thief at a brisk clip, I had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 left my own room unlocked}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
. And had the fabled burglar taken advantage of my carelessness, and made off with my own meager valuables, you might ask?
\par Yes. Yes she had.
\par I haven\rquote t much coin to my name as you know, but the remainder of the allowance the Order granted me in pursuit of this investigation, she took. My entire coin purse was gone, as well as my patents, and the few other bits of investigative material I
\rquote d gathered. Thankfully, I did not have her letter on me. I\rquote m very glad I passed that off to you and Brother Dolus when I did.
\par Most of the rest of what she\rquote d made off with were magazines, some from our library back home, others I\rquote d gathered as I\rquote d traveled, trying to discern anything more I could about this area and how it might relate to her. She had 
also taken my prayer book, and while I\rquote m grateful at least it was not my far older, more valuable, and more personally significant Tome of Faith\'85 that remained untouched, oddly\'85 my prayer book was essentially }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 my}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  journal. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 The loss of it wounds me nearly as much.}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
\par None of my scrawlings concerned the case, or the Heart Thief, or anything significant to the Templar Order. Please do not worry yourself, there. You impressed upon me 
from a young age to commit nothing to writing that could be compromising to the Church, unless it be sealed and sent either up the chain from on holy land, or mailed with an approved Courier, as 
this letter has been. Your caution and the edicts of the Order have been prescient- which I\rquote d imagine is precisely why they are so- in preventing what could have been a disastrous turn in this investigation.
\par My prayer book was more of a personal log of my favorite psalms, prayers, and conversations with God, as well as my musings about my faith, and the meanings behind certain passages. Many of the things I\rquote ve scribbled about in there, I ha
ve only shared with you or the other acolytes, if anyone. It isn\rquote t that any of them are particularly scandalous or defaming\'85 I just think a few of my inner thoughts are profoundly stupid, and not worth sharing. 
\par Still, all of that aside, I cannot work out why she might have taken it. The magazines make sense. She\rquote s clever; she\rquote ll likely deduce they were how I\rquote d known where to look for her. Without my patents and my coin, I\rquote ve had
 trouble traveling or asserting my authority. But why my prayer book? It\rquote s so intensely }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 personal}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  and unconnected from the case- from her- and it\rquote s valueless. Has she simply taken it to hurt me? To offend me?
\par I will bear its\rquote  loss well enough. I\rquote d written it off from the moment I found it missing, and I have more pressing concerns than mourning the lack of it. She\rquote ll probably destroy it when she realizes it provides her
 nothing of value, or simply discard it. It is regrettably gone forever. That\rquote s that.
\par It's not that it doesn\rquote t matter to me. I\rquote ve spent countless hours filling its pages, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 musing on my life}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 , and exploring the nuances of passages from the Tome I seek clarity in understanding. The reality you are burdened with- that I do not avoid Confession, not even through omission or across great d
istances- is a point of pride for me. But we }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 are}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  permitted some
 private thoughts on doctrine. 
\par That book has been a place for me to explore my faith and ask myself questions. It was only ever of any value or importance to me. I can\rquote t imagine what she wants with it.
\par I made haste from the carriage house initially, thinking I might be able to use sightings in town to discern which direction she\rquote d fled in. The road there only}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  forked twice}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  and the woodlands in the lake country are treacherous without a guide. Ad
ditionally with the weather being as miserable with sleet and cold as it has been moving into winter, it didn\rquote t seem likely she\rquote d take off on foot. 
\par But she\rquote d left in the dead of night, and accounts of anyone matching her description leaving town were naug
ht to be found. Only a few night watchmen and a street cleaner were even out at that hour, and not a one had seen anyone traversing the road, other than themselves.
\par That left me to either take to the roads on foot- a useless gesture, although one I still considered- 
or speak to the drivers staying in town, to see whom she may have gotten a ride with. I occupied myself in the interim hours stopping by and rousing the local Parish Priest and his two live-in curates. They hardly knew what to make of me, but ra
llied themselves admirably despite the impossible hour and were willing to hear me out. I met with some expected incredulity considering my story and my lack of papers, but the engraving on my blade and my vestments saw me through, there. I\rquote 
m not certain I would be able to assert my identity or station using similar methods outside the realm of holy men, though. I need my patents. Please tell me where I might go to have new papers drawn up.
\par The Parish Priest, 
Father White, was very kind to me, providing me with an early morning meal and eventually, a mule for my travels. It is all they had to offer, and more than I deserve, but pursuing my investigation any other way at this point would have been impossible. W
ithout my coin, I will be depending on the kindness of similar small country Parishes as I travel, at least until I can make it to Redcoven and a Ministry-affiliated Church.
\par The mule is a tiresome beast and none-too-fond of being ridden, but I\rquote ve managed to wrestle control of her. Somewhat. I returned to the carriage house once dawn rose, to speak to the drivers. One of them, the badger who\rquote 
d been my driver the day before, was eager to be of service. To put it bluntly, I think the man\rquote s life is a b
ore. He was a world-weary man who did not fluster easily. I suppose you see most everything, in his line of work. He took an interest in the dispute the night before
, at least as it served as entertainment for him, and he was very happy to speak with me over his breakfast.
\par He at once dispelled any notion I might have had that she\rquote d taken a carriage, at least any that ran the major routes and had reputable drivers. He could name every one of them, and more importantly, whom had been in town the night before 
or morning thereafter. He assured me no one had left the carriage house yet\'85 there was an abrupt dead man\rquote s turn on the mountain path to the east, and a bridge prone to 
washing out to the west- no one would leave in the dead of night, he assured me. Even extra coin wasn\rquote t worth losing a horse,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  getting lost,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  or worse.
\par I dared not hope she was still in town, hiding herself away or sheltering with an ally. I reserved it as a slim possibility in the back of my mind, but set to canvas the town to find out if the
re were any other means of transport she might have found her way to. And in time, I discovered it.
\par It took a good portion of the day, but I eventually found myself at the doorstep of a family of polecats, a mother and her three children. I\rquote d been referred
 there because apparently the husband of the house was known to have a small, single-person buggy he used to ferry goods back and forth between a town not ten miles away. In areas like this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 ,}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  many tradesmen and families were connected, so a short-range courier was probably a lucrative career. His buggy wasn\rquote t meant for two, but\'85
 a polecat and a lean feline may have been able to squeeze into a seat that had doubtless been designed for canines.
\par And sure enough, when I got there the wife confirmed it. According to her, a wealthy foreign heiress who \lquote may have been runnin\rquote  from a man\rquote , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 in her words}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 , had commissioned her husband for a goodly sum to bring her with him on his trip that morning. Which meant I knew where she was headed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 .}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
\par Unfortunately, my mule }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 had no chance of catching up with a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 buggy. I set out that very night, }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 determined to make up for the time I\rquote d lost somehow, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 in my rush}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  failed to consider two very important things.
\par One- I had no food. Water, I had made sure I\rquote d brought along. The well in town wa
s free, after all. But as for food, I had only the breakfast in my belly from the Parish. I could- nay, should- have stopped there to get a loaf of bread, or anything really, before I\rquote 
d gone. But I was impatient and eager to be hot on her heels again.
\par Two- every hop I\rquote d taken before had been with a driver of some sort. I didn\rquote t know the countryside, and the lake country as it turns out\'85
 is wet. Couple that with a chilling fog and washed-out roads, and late into the night, I had to accept the reality that I was lost. I\rquote d most certainly been riding for more than 10 miles, but I hadn\rquote 
t found the hamlet the buggy rider was bound for.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  I stopped near an old, dry bridge to rest my eyes until dawn light, then set out again.
\par Daylight did not avail me, and I continued to travel the game trails and rustic roads that criss-cross the rugged countryside out here, until it began to sleet. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  Exhausted, fr
}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 ozen}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  to the bone and hopeless to make any more progress in the}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  poor weather}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 , I sheltered in the first haven I\rquote d seen for miles.
\par A decaying barn.
\par And that brings me to the present moment. The loft of the barn turned out to be full of fresh hay, which was a welcome surprise. What\rquote s more, the fact that it\rquote s still in use means I might not be as far from town as I thought. It\rquote s har
dly warm, and the hay is stiff with frost and jagged to the touch, but it is a blessed refuge nonetheless. God was looking out for me.
\par I wish I could serve Him as faithfully as He deserves. But I am constantly grappling with my own poor decisions and failings of character}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
, and I fear I am unworthy of the blessings He grants me}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 .}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  It is a constant trouble.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  
\par I am not certain what possessed me- I couldn\rquote t sleep, that I remember, and I thought to take out the few things she\rquote d left for me in that room. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
Ink and a quill, which was odd, but came in handy penning this letter. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 Three books, all of which were worldly stories, of course. I didn\rquote 
t recognize the titles save one, \lquote The Admiral\rquote s White Claw\rquote . I\rquote ve seen it in the Library at the Abbey before\'85 and yes, I know it\rquote s restricted reading to any of us in the Clergy.
\par But I felt certain the books she\rquote d left me might hold some kind of meaning to her, or to the case. And if there was anything to be gained from investigating them, one worldly work of fiction here or there is hardly going to shake my faith.
\par The next part, I am far more ashamed of.
\par At some point into the night, after having consumed a good deal of the shameless and perversely indulgent story therein- a high seas romance bet
ween an Amur Admiral and a Kadrush snowcat woman apparently, filthy, just pointless tripe- I became of a mind that the only other possession I had of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 Darcy\rquote s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 , the half-empty bottle of wine, might alleviate my hunger and warm my body.
\par I know I am not forbidden alcohol, but}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  I\rquote ve considered a vow of sobriety just based on the evils I\rquote ve seen it concoct in others.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511  I\rquote ve }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 sampled}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
 very little save watered-down beer a few times. And I\rquote ve never liked it. 
\par I think I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 might }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 like wine}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , though}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 . I\rquote ve had it in cooking before, and I\rquote ve sipped on the Abbey wine, but this is\'85 different. It\rquote s some kind of country wine, and it\rquote s thicker, headier, 
with a sweet aftertaste like honey.
\par I lost track of myself, just glad to be putting anything in my stomach, and before I knew it I\rquote d drained what was left in the bottle. That didn\rquote t alarm me until a short while }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
later}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 . I\rquote 
d thought half a bottle of country wine would be no issue for a man of my size, but apparently I do not handle my drink well, or perhaps because I had no meal with it, I don\rquote t know\'85
\par I am completely certain at this moment that I am too inebriated to get down from this loft. I lit the lantern on the lower level before I came up, and I had intended to snuff it out before I slept, but it is no good. My few attempts thus far ha
ve been near-disasters, and drunkenly falling from a hay loft and crushing my skull would be a truly ignoble death.
\par My competence with a quill has not failed me yet, thankfully, so I thought to occupy myself penning this letter. I will make proper penance when 
I am home, but for the time being, know that I prayed for forgiveness for my lack of temperance, and that I am quite beside myself.
\par I am going to try once more for sleep. I will decide}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 whether or not to send this letter once I\rquote 
ve looked it over in the morning.
