\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16069644 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 The sounds of the sea. I\rquote d heard them only
 once in my life. When we\rquote d taken our pilgrimage, all of us at the Abbey. We\rquote d taken a barge from the Crystal Lakes down-river to Leifolk, and I\rquote d gotten to see that big expanse of endless blue. God\rquote 
s wonder, laid out in an impossible vista where heaven and earth meet forever in all directions. The late Admiral Cross once said that only those who\rquote 
ve seen the view from far out to sea, so far that the land recedes beyond the curve, can begin to accept their small and insignificant place in God\rquote s plan. It is a humbling feeling many could benefit from, in his opinion }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid6104500 and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  mine. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid9504224 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 That night, I dreamed of the sea. The loft was cold and my mind troubled and addled by drink, so I slept fitfully, waking often and drifting into
 that faraway place intermittently. The river barge, then the docks we\rquote d walked, Father Helstrom leading the pack of us boys\'85 I saw faces I hadn\rquote t seen for years, lads who\rquote d failed to conform and gone stray, some who\rquote 
d been Ordained before me, and even Nicholas, clutching at my sleeve. Father Helstrom, younger then but you could hardly tell, and still just as stern and commanding.
\par The dreamscape- right, I was dreaming- shifted away from memory. Now the blue, the churning waves, the kiss of the heavens upon the horizon, was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14618886 all}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  I could see. And\'85 a ship? I must have been aboard a ship. A warship, I think. There were canons on the upper deck. No, wait\'85 weren\rquote t they on another, lower deck? I couldn\rquote t remember. I
\rquote d seen very few galleons when I\rquote d been at port, most warships were away at the time, ferrying souls from the crumbling Colonies in the Dark Continent, or defending our waters from pirates.
\par The ship was an amalgam of the pieced-together memories I had of the few warships I\rquote d seen then, all from a distance, illustrations from books, and my own imaginings. I couldn\rquote t work out why I was there, but each man I passed milling ab
out doing tasks I literally couldn\rquote t fill in with any clarity, seemed to know me. No one stopped me from moving through the ship towards some inevitable destination. 
\par I couldn\rquote t remember how I knew where to go, or why I was here, but it was no matter. What mattered was that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14025792 she}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  was waiting for me. That\rquote s right\'85
\par She was hidden away in my cabin, and no one could discover us. No one could know I was sheltering her. Ferrying her to freedom.
\par And perhaps myself? I had not decided yet. To give up all of this, to give up this life, this role that had thus far defined me, all for a woman? An enemy of the state?
\par An enemy\'85 turned lover.
\par When I slipped inside my cabin, the lantern must have been lit low. The light from outside- once blindingly bright, high noon- was subdued here. Like the night had already fallen, somehow.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 And there, waiting for me, the slender slope of her back lit along the edge and broken only by ebon spots, was the subject of my indecision and torment.
\par She did not turn to greet me, the steady rise and fall of her ribs suggesting she slumbered. I approached her, my footsteps soft, and shed my cloak- no, coat- a red coat, trimmed elegantly in gold along\'85 most seams, I imagin
ed. Would I be wearing a cravat, perhaps? A fine vest? I wasn\rquote t sure, but I thought that would look right and proper, so\'85 there they were. I shed them as well, stripping down to my long shirt and breeches, and little more. The thick bracelet I
\rquote d had since I was a child, my only heirloom, slid down to rest at my wrist as I gingerly reached for her, tracing my clawed fingers through the air over her shoulder. 
\par I yearned to touch her, but not while she slept. Not without her permission.
\par And just like that, she
 rolled slowly first to her side, and then her back. And those big, dandelion-colored eyes opened, her muzzle curling into a sleepy smile. Her downy breast rose and fell, covered in soft, indistinct fur that masked her petite, nearly flat chest. I\rquote 
d seen very few nude women over the course of my life, save when I was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14424599 very}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 young and once when a harlot had intentionally unlaced and flashed me and Brother Dolus while we were visiting a nearby city. That woman had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid12716077 sizable}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  breasts, and unrestrained by modesty or laced-up garments, they\rquote d been utterly obscene. But I\rquote 
d never really thought of that memory with anything other than embarrassment. For myself or for her, I wasn\rquote t sure. I was certainly not bewitched by the sight.
\par T
he beautiful spotted cat before me had a more elegant and understated figure, and the lack of a voluptuous chest did her no disservice. Her shoulders were strong and defined for one so slender, her arms stretching up behind her head, back arching as she y
awned, quite intentionally putting herself on display. Her torso flared down into a trim waistline and thickened up again along her heavily spotted thighs, the black marks trickling down into joined paths. The markings wrapped around the soft expanse of
 her inner thighs and drew the eye towards the apex of her legs, where\'85
\par I did not know what I\rquote d see, I wasn\rquote t entirely sure what I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid1783093 wanted}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 to see, and I certainly worried about what that meant. But that was beside the point\'85 something about all of this had begun to feel wrong. The wrongness was like an undertow, pulling at my heels, reminding me it was there and 
would take me in time, but easy enough to ignore for now, now when I was consumed by a blooming want, an all-important }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid16663591 ache}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 \'85
\par She was nuzzling that soft, cool nose of hers into my throat. I could feel her whiskers twitch, her hands gripping at my arms, pulling me to her. I braced my palm into the soft comforter beneath, and moved myself over her on my hands and knees. I
 tried to keep my hips up, but moments later felt them drawn }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14025792 down}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
, in, against her, as if on instinct. I fought the urge valiantly, until with a huff- as if I frustrated her- she wrapped those robust, apparently strong thighs around my waist, and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid6051887 squeezed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 .
\par The sound that left my muzzle was piteously }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5534476 pained}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , moaned out in a stuttering breath, like I\rquote 
d been punched. I could feel the heat of her there, merging with my own, my body driven without thought by some primordial, deeply-ingrained voice that had no language to offer, only }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5534476 want}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5534476 push}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , grind }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5534476 down}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , open her }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5534476 up}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 -
\par The strands of guilt clutched at my ankles and tried to drag me back towards reason, but the overwhelming tide of need snapped each of them as soon as they took hold. If any part of me had the where
withal to stop this, to stop myself, it crumbled when a sound pierced the quiet, freezing my heart mid-beat.
\par A breathless, husky laugh. I blinked to bring the intoxicating scene beneath me into focus. She was staring up at me, eyes half-lidded, eyelids a d
usky gray encased by a frame of gold and black. She was smiling at me like she found my struggles delightful. And the laugh, soft and lyrical and just on the tail end of a sigh, was one I\rquote d heard before. So uniquely her, it was unmistakable.
\par I\rquote d known it was her, of course. It was always her.
\par That current of wrongness returned, stronger than before. But then she slipped her fingers through the fur at the scruff of my neck, and leaned up to kiss me.
\par I\rquote d never kissed anyone before. I had no idea how it was meant to feel\'85 but what my mind supplanted was an indescribable warmth and fulfillment of that want that had been hollowing me out, feeding me like no delights of the earth could
, seeping into me from her. I was the parched roots of a dying tree and she the rain. Every empty place in me was full, and I wondered if she was so fulfilled by it as I. 
\par My answer came soon enough. Her fingers sunk into my hip, the prick of claws tugging at my flesh, and without hesitance, she pulled our bodies flush. Something }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid5594883 gave}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  between us, and we were joined, her gasp puffing out into my muzzle.
\par It seemed impossible that our bodies could fit so perfectly to each other, that she could accommodate my cumbersome weight, let alone our overall difference in size in\'85 all areas\'85 but she was hardly acting as though she were in }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid10496232 pain}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 . She was not soldiering through this in obligation, or carrying out a duty she was bound to by covenant. 
No one compelled her actions but she. We were at sea, as untethered from our duties and as free to pursue our wants as could possibly be imagined. She was not just tolerating this for my sake, she was as lost to this need as I was. 
\par It was profane. It was heresy. It was rapture unlike any I\rquote d ever known.
\par She continued to kiss me and clutch me to her as though }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid15498720 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  were the one being claimed, as though }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid15498720 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  were the one fulfilling }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid1783093 her}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  desires. It was ludicrous- wishful thinking, maybe- but I felt so certain, somehow.
\par The warmth was burning through my body like a fever, driving me restlessly to writhe and dig down further into the comforter, to snap my hips down, down, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid9576469 more}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  and somehow deeper, chasing something\'85 becoming fully one with her, maybe\'85 God, I yearned, what was it I was after, again? I felt like I was being pulled apart by need
, drawn down to some singular point of sharp, trembling desire, but I could not fathom what more I could possibly need }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid12911759 from}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  her. From this. How could this moment- which already felt as though it could shake me apart- get any better?
\par Her tongue lolled out between her lower teeth, her eyes slipping to crescents, and she }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid12796503 purred}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 \'85}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  oh, Lord, I had forgotten felines did that when they were in pleasure\'85 God help me\'85 I was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid12796503 pleasuring}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  her-!
\par The roaring surge of elation that washed over me struck like a crashing wave. It felt like it had jogged my }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid8400487 soul}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  loose from my body, the soft-hued image of beauty beneath me slipping away to hazy white, blinding. I rode out the overwhelming and terrifying rush, helpless against the current so succumbing to it instead,
 until landing heavily back in the restraints of physical form, the waking world cruelly asserting itself. 
\par It was black and dim blue, my breath was coming out in haggard puffs, my face and muzzle crushed to hard, splintered wood. My thin blanket and cloak were tangled up around me like I\rquote d fallen throug
h a tent, bunched up around my feet and between my legs. My body }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid9576469 ached}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 with the cold I had been unable to feel until just now, an accrued punishment from hours spent ignoring my condition. The barn was nearly as cold as the air outside drifting in through the slats, and I was swaddled poorly for the elements.
\par Distantly, the mule brayed. And farther off, a rooster called the morning to rise. Slivers of blue light fell in through the decaying roof, the only illumination up here in the loft.
\par I groaned, long and pathetic-sounding, and dragged a snort wetly through my nose. I couldn\rquote t breathe through it, what had been a niggling cold was now settling in for good, so I\rquote 
d been breathing the frigid air all night with my mouth open. My tongue and teeth felt dry, my throat stung and cried out for water, and my eyes burned. And I felt\'85 strange, otherwise. My heart was thudding too quickly for having been at rest
, my nerves stinging, washing me with a suddenly exhausted sensation. I probably hadn\rquote t gotten much sleep, but-
\par Trying to sit up was a mistake. I yelped and bent double, clutching down between my legs where I\rquote d nearly crushed myself while rolling over. I knew immediately I\rquote d spilled my seed in my sleep again. It was easy enough to tell 
by the wet sensation between my thighs, but I hadn\rquote t expected I\rquote d still be so }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid4066017 prominent}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 down there, now that it was over and all. It had always receded before.
\par Unable to control myself, I flung my paw down between my legs and gripped the outline of my member. I pulled my palm away a second later as though seared by a hot pan, but I\rquote d gotten enough of a feel to realize\'85
\par My knot was out. And the slick pooling at the front of my button-up was still warm. This had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid3767806 just}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  happened.

\par And it was so painfully clear what the cause was.
\par I\rquote d had these morning issues for many years now, up to and including the rare times I spilled in my sleep, long before I\rquote d ever met her. I had tried not to connect the two in my mind, even though all the signs
 were there. But to do so at this point would be meaningless. I would know I was lying to myself. What\rquote s more, God would know.
\par  Lord forgive me, I had been\'85 
\par \'85I had been\'85
\par My paw fell, trembling, hesitant, to feel the shape of my manhood, still restrained by my clothing, but absurdly obvious. It was distress
ingly foreign to me, for a piece of my own body. The contours were unfamiliar, forbidden, a filthy secret kept restrained by my sheath and my self-control. But the one could only hold what it fit, and the other was waning, apparently. And now this 
manifestation of sickening desire, this }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid9393457 thing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  was rising from its confines with more and more frequency, 
and forcing me to reckon with it.
\par I hated it, and hated myself. I wanted nothing more than to disown it until the appropriate time, when I was married, and all of this would make more sense. There were rules for marriage. I would know what to do then. All of this would be so much less }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid3942883 confusing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 .
\par I still had it gripped there, beneath my clothing, and what had begun as a growl in my throat yielded to become the whine I truly felt. I was so held captive by my petulant anger that I couldn\rquote 
t even control the noises leaving my muzzle, anymore. Seized by a sudden desire to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid7281334 make this all stop}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
, somehow, to reassert some measure of control over this rogue part of my body, I squeezed my palm around the knot. Until it hurt.
\par Oddly, it was when the pain began to first prickle that I was able to let out a breath. Stuttered, long-winded\'85 slow. But it felt so immensely relieving. I focused on that feeling, closing my eyes and chasing it down a wobbly path towards restoration.

\par I began to relax, and loosened my grip, until I could not easily feel the shape of it any more. I slumped back against the beam I\rquote d slept beside and let my thoughts drift. They inevitably returned to the panicked realizations from earlier,
 but I was calmer now. I could think on this rationally.
\par Undeniably\'85. I had been\'85 sinning, with the Heart Thief, in my dream. Fornicating. With her.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14574257 Darcy}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 .
\par I tipped my ears back, feeling them brush the wood behind me. I had begun to think of her by name, which was not advisable. Beyond the fact that it was certainly a cover- she would never have given me 
her real name- I was showing signs of succumbing to one of the most warned-against failures of temperance a Templar could fall prey to: personalizing their relationship with a target.
\par I was compromised. I knew the moment the words came to mind, instantly, that they were true. The only possible thing to do at this time to protect the integrity of this case, and my own soul, would be to turn in everything I\rquote 
d found, and recuse myself from it. I had given in to the very weakness I\rquote d once scoffed at other men for letting rule them.
\par I\rquote d been seduced by her.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16069644 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 \tab Every one of the scant few words she\rquote 
d spoken to me, every brush of her body against mine
, every brief interaction, had been fodder for the recesses of my mind to pour over, for weeks now. And I could not help but linger on those most damning snippets. The lilt of her voice as she teased me, the whisper of her nimble hands over my body as she
\rquote d removed my harness- intentional, certainly- and the warmth of her thigh, purposefully positioned as it was, supporting my head on the bench near the dovecote. Even the way she laughed- it was a lurid display, all of it!
 She knew precisely what she was doing; every one of her carefully-planned and flawlessly-delivered flirtations tempted me down a road to ruin. And God help me, if not in the waking world, in my dreams I was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14574257 allowing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  it to take hold. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16069644 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 It made some sense, really\'85 my mind was 
untethered and more vulnerable when at rest. A perfect opportunity for dark forces to sink their fangs into the weak spots in my soul.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16069644 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  \tab Well, I wouldn\rquote 
t let it happen. So long as I resisted, so long as I held to the clarity of mind God had granted me to see me through the trials of sin, I would not falter. I might stumble. The dreams were a bump in the road. One I
 would find a way to suppress, and make penance for with Confession and\'85 whatever my Priest deemed necessary to quench the fires.
\par \tab Ciraberos wanted me to fail, in this task. The hell hounds would keep nipping at my heels and looking for purchase with their
 jaws, not just now but for the rest of my life. They would always be there, legion, dragging me towards damnation. This was a test. A trial. How could I even be considering giving up the case? That would mean being complacent in the face of sin, acceptin
g I could }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14187586 not}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  overcome it.
\par \tab It would mean she\rquote d beaten me. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16069644 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 And I was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14574257 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  close. The Order may not have believed or may not have taken note of my reports yet, but I knew what I\rquote d seen and heard. She couldn
\rquote t get lucky forever. Eventually, if I kept up the pursuit, I\rquote d have her cornered.
\par Felines were all about ambush and surprise. But I had endurance, and my pack. She was moving through }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid14224105 our}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 society, and canines helped canines, first. I just had to run her down, and out of options.
\par My ears twitched. In the damp, quiet air of the morning, carried across the nearby meadow, I caught the first hint of a most welcome sound. Salvation.
\par Clattering wagon wheels.
\par It sounded, as I listened more closely, like little more than a hay wagon or a mule cart. Probably a farmer, someone lowly. The clattering tinny noises reminded me of a milk cart, which it may well have been.
\par Whoever it was, they\rquote d know how to get to town.
\par I shuffled up to a sitting position quickly, and once again regretted it, but for a different reason this time. I palmed my forehead, rubbing my finger pads down the seam of my skull, groaning. 
\par I glanced to my left, where the discarded bottle from the night before lay prone. It seemed to be laughing at me. Squinting, I noticed a haphazardly-strewn small stack of paper weighted down beneath it, scrawled with writing. It was my paper\'85
 and my handwriting. I couldn\rquote t recall exactly what I may have written last night, though.
\par The wagon was still distant, but certainly coming this way. I had a moment or two to look over the papers while I hazily found my few possessions. It seemed like I\rquote d penned a letter to Father Helstrom, which\'85
 made sense. But Lord, what had I written?! I was normally prone to rambling when I sent word to my Father, but he would }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid2632235 never}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  forgive me for a letter near six pages long. I\rquote d need to look the small novel over later, and pen something more concise.
\par Speaking of novels\'85
\par I gathered up the three books while I got together my things, and took note of just how far my tassel was into the first of the three. I\rquote d read through nearly two thirds of the first book last night? I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid2632235 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  a voracious reader, but still\'85 considering how blurry the night was, how much of it would I really remember? Might be worth another look.

\par A fleeting glimpse of the dream I\rquote d had suddenly asserted itself, and I lurched with a realization. I hadn\rquote t just been dreaming about her.
\par I\rquote d been dreaming about the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid2632235 novel}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 too. I\rquote d\'85 transposed us into the story, she and I.
\par God. I needed Confession.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid13903511 Cillian Rathborne.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 I stared down at the name on the elegantly-printed note in my hand, uncertain what to make of it. It was a Pedigree name, or at least adjacent, a branch family, perhaps. I\rquote 
d heard of at least one other household with the name, although I couldn\rquote t be certain there were any men amongst their number named \lquote Cillian\rquote 
. They were a family out of Amurfolk, if memory served, which meant they were prominent enough to have connections in the Capital, but little more than that. Most Pedigree families had a city h
ome there, or at least a tangential connection to family who did. As Templar, we were expected to keep studied on major names and households that played any role in politics, the military, or Church dealings. This family must have skirted the line there, 
because I had a good head for names and theirs was only vaguely ringing a bell.
\par Probably a family who\rquote d had some kind of scandal in the past, something to take note of in the ledgers, but no important connections otherwise.
\par The note was envelope-sized, printed on durable rag stock and hand-signed by a banker from Amurfolk and the man in question. He had a fine hand, or at least the seneschal signing }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid4669638 for}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  him did. It was a custom note, not one of those new fixed denomination bank notes the King had issued to finance the
 failed war on the Dark Continent. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid201512 Paper}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  money. I couldn\rquote t see it catching on.
\par The subject and recipient lines were blank, or at least had been at one point, and had been filled in recently judging by the fresh ink. And, of course, the account of the man who was showing it to me.
\par I stared across the counter of the carriage house I\rquote d found myself in, eyeing
 the nervous mutt, keeping my expression firm, but neutral. Father Helstrom had spent years training me on affect, when dealing with every rung of society either high or low. There was a time to throw your autho
rity around and a time to rein it back in. Thus far this man had been tripping over himself trying to be accommodating, from the moment I\rquote 
d come into his place of business. The whiff of fear and the obvious tells in his body language suggested to me he\rquote d had trouble with the law before, or the Church. Or knew someone who had.
\par Or, he suspected he\rquote d done something wrong in accepting this note. A guilty conscience was a rank smell you got a nose for after a while. I\rquote d had more than enough experience with my own. 
\par But all of that aside, he\rquote d done the right thing in handing this over to me. So, after scanning it once more, I made an effort to soften my features. \'93And you\rquote re certain\'85 the woman who used this\'85 she was feline? Black fur?
 Large, straight ears? Lean of build, demure and proper in her speech?\'94
\par The man was already nodding. \'93Yes, yes, all of those things, Brother. Only, ah\'85\'94
\par I gave him some space to continue, then pressed with an easy, gentle tone. \'93Go on. Please. You\rquote re not in any trouble, I promise.\'94
\par I was lucky this man had recognized my vestments. He hadn\rquote t once asked for my patents. To be fair, I was also armed. That helped.
\par \'93Well, ah\'85\'94 he stammered, \'93\'85 the description\rquote s }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid6359792 uncanny}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , only\'85 they weren\rquote 
t no woman.\'94
\par \'93Ah,\'94 I said with understanding. \'93Well, she may have been wearing britches. She\rquote s a criminal, and fond of disguise.\'94
\par The man\rquote s eyes widened, crinkling his spotted brow. \'93Really?! Aye, well sh\rquote fooled me alright, then. And we don\rquote t take notes from women, so believe me when I tell you, she had me sold. Man-like posture, he- I mean she- 
carried her own bags like it weren\rquote t nothin\rquote . Deeper voice\rquote n on most ladies, too, if I remember it right.\'94 He leaned an arm over the counter, looking intrigued now. \'93And you sayin\rquote  she\rquote s some kinda\rquote 
 criminal, then? Wot\rquote d she do?\'94
\par \'93Well, for one,\'94 I held up the note, \'93she\rquote s passing false notes. This is stolen.\'94
\par The man slumped. \'93Am I out the whole lot, then? I checked the seal an\rquote everything.\'94
\par \'93The Church does not perpetuate theft, sir,\'94 I assured him. \'93You\rquote ll just need to wait for us to send along a courier to reimburse you for it.\'94
\par \'93I\rquote ve already paid the coachman,\'94 he sighed. \'93She got one of my best, too, damn it all. Private, swift box.\'94
\par \'93The Order appreciates your compliance,\'94 I said by rote, not paying much mind to his grousing.  My eyes were pinned to the note. Specifically the fact that there were }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid12322240 three}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  distinct sets of handwriting on it. The signature from the house that had written the note, which could 
be from the Lord himself, if not a seneschal. The carriage house owner- he\rquote d confirmed that himself.
\par And hers.
\par Her hand was distinctive. Elegant, educated for certain, but unusual. The way she accented the flare of the quill along the edges of letters lent the writing an almost foreign appearance. If I had to guess, it suggested someone who\rquote 
d either written or read a different language before learning Amur. The residual stylization was the sort you\rquote d see when Amuraic had begun transitioning to our modern alphabet. Or Huudari writers who were able to pen in Amur as a second language. I
\rquote d actually read aged texts copied by ancient monks where you could still see the old-world influence. It was subtle, but consistent. I felt certain I was right. And it fit so well into the patchwork tapestry I had of her thus far. 
\par She was fluent and literate in our language, and not in the way a commoner would be, with best guesses at spelling and bungled structure. She was as schooled as I had been at least, and we received a rigorous education from the monks at Ebon Gables. What
\rquote s more, she had no trace of accent in her speaking voice that I could detect. She was very well comfortable and at home in our culture, and probably had been for some time. But she seemed young, which meant she\rquote d likely grown up here.

\par Where? Why? And how did a foreign feline }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid11098214 woman}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  receive the kind of education she\rquote d clearly had? 

\par Oh, she had really given me something to work with, here. Not only was this snippet, this small error she\rquote d made giving me reason for confirmation of many of the guesses I\rquote d already had about her\'85
 it was another piece of evidence with her handwriting on it. And another crime, regardless whether it was stolen or given freely.
\par Stolen would be less complicated. The alternative was worse. If this man was some accomplice of hers- which would once again make sense, we\rquote d suspected for a long time she might have help or information from within elite society- 
that would mean breaching Pedigree circles in the investigation, and I was not an Inquisitor }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid668844 yet}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
. I was only recently Ordained as a Journeyman Templar in fact; I didn\rquote t even have a house assignment. Inquisition within the ranks of the elite was delicate work. That\rquote s why the Ministry existed within the Templar order at all. 
\par If it got that far, I\rquote d need Father Helstrom\rquote 
s backing. The idea of going up against a Pedigree House was daunting, somehow more so than the Heart Thief herself. Investigation, I at least felt I had a head for. Bureaucracy was another matter.
\par  I left the carriage house renewed in my conviction, if not my vigor. She was bound for Redcoven. I could have guessed as much. I
t was the largest city in the area and a good place for a criminal to go to ground. She likely realized if she kept hopping around the small towns off the main trade road, she\rquote 
d leave an easy-to-follow trail. In a larger city, she could scrub her tracks. Blend in. Lose me. 
\par What I doubted she realized was that Redcoven had a Ministry Office. My Order was headquartered in most major cities, and in fact I\rquote d done some of my missionary work there, when I was thirteen. 
Training with more seasoned Templar, one of them an Inquisitor himself, if memory served. They worked with the young from time to time as part of their own missionary service. I\rquote d do the same someday.
\par She must have at least considered the chance that I\rquote d follow her here and get my hands on this note. If not, that was a mistake. And severely underestimated me. But either way, it spoke to her emotional state. She\rquote d paid for the fast
est coach she could find here. Doing so was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid9180840 expensive}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , the sum on the note was obscene. Likely why she
\rquote d had to use it. She wasn\rquote t carrying enough coin to pay for a service so lavish.
\par Sloppy.
\par Maybe even\'85 scared.
\par I got an odd twisting in my gut when I considered the idea that I, personally, was scaring a woman. I didn\rquote 
t like it. But Brother Dolus had assured me many times- these initial twinges of moral strife when we first began doing our jobs in earnest were reflexive, a product of misguided but well-intentioned sympathy that we\rquote 
d shake off our fur like frost once we became weathered to the work. God gave us a compass in our soul, and if we looked inwards long enough, we\rquote d feel it guide us true. The feelings I was
 contending with right now were surface-level anxieties based on an immature view of the world. God would relieve me of their burden in time.  
\par Darcy- the Heart Thief- was not just a thief. She was a murderer, and the lowliest kind of Heretic. She\rquote d slain our own, two young Templar who\rquote d only been trying to protect our holy relic while it was being transported
. I wanted to return the Heart of Faith obviously, but more importantly, I wanted their souls to be untethered from the shame of their failure. They c
ould not ascend until their bodies were laid to rest in the earth, and the Order would not do that until their task had been completed.
\par I hadn\rquote t known either of them, but I knew the trials of faith and physical hardship they had endured within the Order throughout their adolescence
 to attain their salvation, and ascend. To have all of that denied, to be stymied between death and rebirth, not to mention to be snuffed out so }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid2102380 young}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 , at all\'85
\par Whoever could do that was filth. And they did not deserve my sympathy. They deserved swift and brutal justice. And if the Order deemed it necessary- death.
\par I made it back out onto the street and nearly to the mule when that train of thought reduced me to a doubled-over stagger against the nearest wall. My shoulder thudded in
to the cedar siding of the feed store, startling the shop keep inside, who I saw gaping at me through the window. I had only barely missed stumbling into the window itself.
\par My ribs constricted, squeezed tight by a spasm and then the inevitable wheezing cough that followed. It persisted long enough that the ragged end led to an encore, and then another. Damn this cold\'85
 I was in the first or second day of it, hard to tell when it had truly taken hold, but it had me in its clutches now. My throat felt burned a
nd parched no matter how much water I drank, my body was aching and sluggish, and the cough would keep me up through the night, I was certain. Especially since I\rquote d have to spend it on the road again. I was hungry, too, and lightheaded. I\rquote 
d beg a meal at the local Church again, but Redcoven was close and I\rquote d already be losing time on the mule. 
\par All of that aside, though\'85 what had my chest in its vicelike grip was not the illness }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid3154932 or}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
 the hunger. It was a thought, an image of the future, imagined in detail, in my overactive mind. 
\par The Heart Thief, hanging at the gallows. As she inevitably would, once she was tried and convicted.
\par I swallowed, steadying my breathing. I had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid3154932 never}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  imagined
 myself to be this delicate. Certainly, I had not been tested by the fires of war, as Father Helstrom had. And I had not the maturity and mental fortitude that more aged, experienced men like Brother Dolus possessed. Nor was I foolish enough to believe th
at rigorous arms training was commensurate to combat experience in the field. I had never had reason to draw steel to defend my life, or ever with any real intention of harming another. Let alone killing someone.
\par We were prepared to bring the Lord\rquote s Justice to bear, if need be. But in my case, the need had not yet arisen. 
\par I had been pursuing this investigation for months now, all while knowing the likely outcome if I were successful. It had been simpler, more straightforward, when there wasn\rquote t a face attached to it all. But that shouldn\rquote 
t have mattered. I could not serve as an arbiter of The Word if I was unprepared to handle death. Even something as horrific as public execution, if sanctioned by the powers that be both heavenly and earthly, was part of my d
uty. I had to be able to sever the knee-jerk emotions like this, to step back and divorce my passions from the whole of it. This was the danger in empathizing with a target.
\par I needed to see her as less of a person. I just wasn\rquote t sure how. The more and more I learned about her, the harder that was becoming. 
\par I didn\rquote t yet know how to restructure my mind to work this way. I was too inexperienced, and too led by my passions. But my passions were what had driven me so far in this case, and\'85 it was hard to know h
ow to cut them off in one area of life, but not others. 
\par I wished, momentarily, that I\rquote d had more talks with Father Helstrom about this very issue. The only senior in my life who\rquote d ever been particularly instructive on the methodology to retrain your emoti
onal gut reactions was Brother Dolus, and I think he and I were cut from different cloth. To eschew the things he had, the connections he\rquote d never have outside the Order, in pursuit of his }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid16654518 service}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  to the Order, you had to possess a more reserved countenance. Monks were, figuratively speaking, a different breed.
\par I ground my teeth together, straightened my posture, and elected for now to bury it down deep- this rumbling thunder of doubt and dread. I had a job to do.
\par And if I couldn\rquote t stomach the thought of being responsible for }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid16654518 this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  woman\rquote 
s death- the thief, the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid11294634 murderer}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  who had desecrated the holiest of holy, violated the very fabric 
of our faith, and killed my brethren to do it\'85
\par \'85 then I was not fit to be an Inquisitor. I would go so far as to say, I was not fit to be a Templar, in fact.
\par I could be prone to bouts of melodrama, I knew this. But it was not a stretch to say- this had now become a test of my conviction. The entirety of our Order was tasked with bringing the Heart Thief to justice if we could appre
hend her. And perhaps the chain of command above me may not have realized how close I was to doing just that, but }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid11294634 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644  knew. And I\rquote d always know.
\par I wasn\rquote t about to let a petty illness, hunger, or my own fainthearted feelings on execution prevent me from doing my duty. God would fill my heart with the comfort of the righteous when all was said and done. 
\par I remembered her words once again, as I readied the mule for travel. I may as well have transcribed them, for all the times I\rquote d considered every syllable.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid11800051 \lquote Do not follow me. I won\rquote t be tortured and locked away by your Church. Next time, if need be\'85 I will defend myself.\rquote }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 God, how I hoped she would. It would make all of this so much easier.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644\charrsid11800051 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f49\fs36\insrsid16069644 
